<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theophylact I, Count of Tusculum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 925</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theophylact I, Count of Tusculum, was a Roman nobleman who dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in the early tenth century. Holding the offices of vestararius, magister militum and senator, he and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-theophilos">Theodora</a> controlled papal appointments, and their daughter Marozia continued the family's power. The period of their influence is seen as the lowest point of papal authority.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theophylact was born before 864, and his origins are unknown and debated. He may have descended from an earlier Theophylact, son of Gregory, the nomenclator of Pope John VIII, who first sided with Pope Formosus and later with his opponents. He held Tusculum, a small hill town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, as its hereditary count, and he first appears in a document of 901 as palatine iudex, a palace judge or leader of the militia, of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-blind">Louis the Blind</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the emperor returned to Provence in 902, Theophylact stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at the head of a body of soldiers. He took a leading part in the overthrow of Antipope Christopher in January 904 and very likely ordered his killing in prison later that year. Allied with Duke Alberic I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, he secured the election of Pope Sergius III, under whom he became sacri palatii vestararius and magister militum and effectively took control of the city. Sometime between the end of Sergius's pontificate and the start of John X's, the nobility elected him Roman consul, and from then on every pope was chosen after he had picked him.</p><p>He shared his rule to a large degree with his wife Theodora, styled senatrix and serenissima vestaratrix. At her suggestion he chose Anastasius III and Lando, and in 914 he backed John X, whom chroniclers called her lover. Theophylact worked closely with John X, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-i-of-italy">Berengar I of Italy</a> and fought beside the pope against the Saracens at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> in 915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Theophylact remained the pope's chief political support until his death. His daughter Marozia married Alberic I, and their son Alberic II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 932 to 954; his heirs, the Tusculani, rivalled the Crescentii for control of the city and were the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna family</a>. Modern historians believe that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liutprand-of-cremona">Liutprand of Cremona</a>, an opponent of the family, exaggerated Theodora's influence. Later writers, following his moral tone, called the period the pornocracy or Rule of the Harlots, a time modern historians name the saeculum obscurum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theophylact I of Tusculum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=915" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Theophylact I, Count of Tusculum"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=915">What was here in 915 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
